  "type": "mc",
  "prompt": "The diagram shows a shape made of arcs of three circles, each with radius $R$. The centres of the circles lie on the same straight line, and the middle circle passes through the centres of the other two circles. What is the perimeter of the shape?",
  "image": "qimages/ukmt/pink-2019/q14.webp",
  "choices": [
   {
    "key": "A",
    "text": "$2\\pi R$"
   },
   {
    "key": "B",
    "text": "$\\frac{5\\pi R}{3}$"
   },
   {
    "key": "C",
    "text": "$\\frac{10\\pi R}{3}$"
   },
   {
    "key": "D",
    "text": "$2\\pi R\\sqrt3$"
   },
   {
    "key": "E",
    "text": "$\\frac{4\\pi R}{3}$"
   }
  ],
  "answer": "C",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Count the arcs",
    "body": "Join each centre to the points where the circles meet. Every circle passes through the centres of its neighbours, so the triangles formed are equilateral and all the angles are $60^\\circ$.\n\nEach outer circle loses an arc of $120^\\circ$ (one third of its circumference) where it overlaps the middle circle, so $\\frac23$ of each outer circle remains. The middle circle contributes the two arcs at top and bottom of $60^\\circ$ each, i.e. $\\frac13$ of its circumference. Total $\\frac23+\\frac23+\\frac13=\\frac53$ circumferences.\n\nPerimeter $=\\frac53\\times2\\pi R=\\frac{10\\pi R}{3}$.\n\n**Answer: (C)**"
   }
  ],
  "topics": [
   "geometry"
  ],
  "difficulty": 2,
  "duplicateOf": null,
  "reviewNote": null
 },
