  "blankPoints": null,
  "wrongPoints": null,
  "timeMinutes": null,
  "type": "mc",
  "prompt": "The integers from 1 to 99 are written in ascending order without spaces. The sequence of digits is then grouped into triples of digits:\n\n$$123456789101112\\ldots979899\\to(123)(456)(789)(101)(112)\\ldots(979)(899).$$\n\nWhich of the following is not one of the triples?",
  "image": null,
  "choices": [
   {
    "key": "A",
    "text": "(222)"
   },
   {
    "key": "B",
    "text": "(434)"
   },
   {
    "key": "C",
    "text": "(464)"
   },
   {
    "key": "D",
    "text": "(777)"
   },
   {
    "key": "E",
    "text": "(888)"
   }
  ],
  "answer": "D",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Find where the triples start",
    "body": "The 9 one-digit numbers use 9 digits, so the triples start at positions $1,4,7,10,\\dots$ (positions $\\equiv1 \\pmod 3$). The tens digit of the two-digit number $n$ is at position $2n-10$ and its units digit is at position $2n-9$.\n\nA triple starts at the tens digit of $n$ when $2n-10\\equiv1\\pmod 3$, that is $n\\equiv1\\pmod3$. It then reads: tens digit of $n$, units digit of $n$, tens digit of $n+1$.\n- $n=22$: (222). $n=43$: (434). $n=46$: (464). $n=88$: (888). All are possible triples.\n\nA triple starts at the units digit of $n$ when $n\\equiv2\\pmod3$; it reads: units digit of $n$, tens and units digits of $n+1$. For $n=77\\equiv 2$ this gives (778) (and $n=76\\equiv1$ gives (767)). So the digits 7,7,7 never form a triple.\n\n**Answer: (D) (777)**"
   }
  ],
  "topics": [
   "patterns",
   "number-theory"
  ],
  "difficulty": 3,
  "duplicateOf": null,
  "reviewNote": null
 },
